      "problem": "For the function\n\n$$\nG(s)=\\frac{1}{s(s+3)},\\,s>-3\n$$\n\nfind the inverse Laplace transform of $G(s),$ given that\n$L\\{{e}^{at}\\}=\\frac{1}{s-a},\\text{ }s>a.$",
      "worked_solution": "Notice that we can write $G(s)$ as \n\n$$\nG(s)=\\frac{1}{s(s+3)}=\\frac{F(s)}{s},\n$$\n\nwhere $F(s)=\\frac{1}{s+3}.$\nAlso, we know that\n\n$$\nL\\{{e}^{at}\\}=\\frac{1}{s-a}.\n$$\n\nThus, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}\\{F(s)\\} & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s+3}\\} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s-(-3)}\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we know that if $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s)$, then\n\n$$\nL\\{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ\\}=\\frac{F(s)}{s}.\n$$\n\nUsing the property above in reverse, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}\\{G(s)\\} & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{F(s)}{s}\\} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{-3τ}\\,\\text{d}τ \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3τ}\\,{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(1-{e}^{-3t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
