      "dom_id": "question-330854",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "For the function\n\n$$\nG(s)=\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}},\\,s>0\n$$\n\nfind the inverse Laplace transform of $G(s),$ given that\n$L\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t)\\}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}.$",
      "worked_solution": "Notice that we can write $G(s)$ as \n\n$$\nG(s)=\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}=\\frac{F(s)}{s},\n$$\n\nwhere $F(s)=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}.$\nAlso, we know that\n\n$$\nL\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t)\\}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}.\n$$\n\nThus, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}\\{F(s)\\} & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}\\} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we know that if $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s)$, then\n\n$$\nL\\{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ\\}=\\frac{F(s)}{s}.\n$$\n\nUsing the property above in reverse, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}\\{G(s)\\} & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{F(s)}{s}\\} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi τ)\\,\\text{d}τ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
